WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.900
Thanks for joining us. This is Omni View, episode 127 for July 29th, 2026. Multiple perspectives, one briefing. The day's biggest stories from around the world, what happened, how different viewpoints frame it, so you can decide for yourself. Russia charges telegrams founder with aiding terrorism and issues in international arrest warrant, raising questions about platform liability worldwide. Russian authorities have charged Pavel Durov, the founder of the

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:59.780
messaging app Telegram with aiding terrorism and placed him on an international wanted list. The move follows earlier legal actions against the company in Russia. Telegram is used by hundreds of millions of people globally for private and public communication. Russian officials frame the charges as necessary to curb the use of encrypted platforms by groups they designate as threats, pointing to Telegram's role in coordinating activities they view as illegal. The AP News

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:29.740
report details how the charges tie into broader Russian efforts to monitor online activity during the Ukraine conflict. Western governments and digital rights organizations have often criticized similar Russian cases as attempts to extend domestic control over global communication tools. Reuters coverage of regional security notes parallel steps by other governments to regulate messaging services. Durov has previously resisted demands for backdoors or user data in

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:59.720
multiple countries, arguing that weakening encryption harms ordinary users more than it helps security services. Everything turns on whether the charges target specific messages tied to attacks or rest on the general refusal to install backdoors. Russian authorities maintain that messaging services must cooperate with lawful requests when their platforms are used to organize attacks or evade detection, because the alternative leaves security agencies without tools again.

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:19.400
This is the case against documented threats. Civil liberties groups and technology companies counter that forcing broad access or data handover creates systemic vulnerabilities that authoritarian governments can exploit against dissidents and journalists, and that targeted warrants on specific accounts already provide a narrower path.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:27.980
What happens next, the first compliance deadlines and any extradition requests will show how far the charges reach beyond Russia's borders.

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:37.740
Shifting now to a development in the Pacific, New Zealand announced plans to acquire strike drones as part of a broader effort to modernize its armed forces.

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:53.060
The purchase is intended to improve surveillance and response capabilities in the country's maritime region. Reuters reports the acquisition forms one element of a multi-year defense review that also includes upgrades to naval and air assets.

00:02:54.060 --> 00:03:01.180
Defense officials in Wellington describe the drones as a practical way to monitor vast ocean areas with limited personnel.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:10.700
Neighboring countries in the Pacific have expanded similar unmanned systems in recent years, prompting New Zealand to keep pace with regional military trends.

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:18.700
Some domestic voices have questioned whether the spending aligns with the country's traditional emphasis on peacekeeping rather than offensive equipment.

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:30.040
Reuters coverage highlights how the decision responds to shifting security assessments in the South Pacific, where several governments now integrate drone fleets into routine patrols.

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:36.420
What happens next, the multi-year review will determine the scale and timing of the first drone deliveries.

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:49.900
Next, an announcement from India's energy sector. NTPC India's biggest electricity generator said it will invest $178 billion through 2037 to expand capacity.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:04:01.280
The plan covers both renewable and conventional sources. Reuters notes the investment spans more than a decade and will be financed through a mix of internal funds and external borrowing.

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:07.080
Government statements link the spending to rising electricity demand from industry and households.

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:13.140
Energy analysts note that meeting the target will require steady policy support and financing.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:19.960
Environmental groups have urged faster renewable deployment within the same budget to limit emissions growth.

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:29.400
Reuters reporting places the figure against India's official target of reaching 500 gigawatts of non-fossil fuel capacity by 2030,

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:33.660
showing how the NTPC program fits into that national goal.

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:40.840
What happens next, actual spending will depend on future budgets and technology choices across the coming decade.

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:50.740
Turning to company results in the technology supply chain, SK Highnecks reported its highest ever quarterly profit driven by demand for AI chips.

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:56.960
Yet the figure fell short of analyst expectations. Shares dropped 10% in early trading.

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:03.320
Reuters states the profit rose 557% from the same quarter a year earlier.

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:08.720
Company executives attributed the result to strong memory chip sales for data centers.

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:14.400
Market participants appear to have priced in even faster growth, leading to the sell-off.

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:23.300
The outcome illustrates how high expectations in the AI supply chain can produce sharp price moves even on positive underlying demand.

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:32.800
Reuters notes that SK Highnecks remains one of the three largest suppliers of high bandwidth memory chips used in advanced AI training systems.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:40.480
What happens next, upcoming technology earnings reports will give further signals on whether demand continues to outpace forecasts.

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:45.480
Meanwhile, markets showed little movement ahead of a key policy decision.

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:55.140
Major stock indexes showed little movement as investors waited for the US Federal Reserve's policy announcement and upcoming technology earnings reports.

00:05:56.060 --> 00:06:01.480
Reuters describes the session as one of the quietest in recent weeks across major exchanges.

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:07.680
Traders cited uncertainty over interest rate paths as the main reason for muted activity.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:14.780
Technology shares which have led recent gains are expected to provide clearer signals once results are released.

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:21.500
The quiet session reflects a market that prefers to see concrete data before committing to larger positions.

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:29.300
Reuters' market rap notes that futures pricing shows investors now expect the Fed to hold rates steady at the upcoming meeting.

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:35.920
What happens next, the Federal Reserve announcement itself will set the tone for trading in the days after.

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:38.760
Now, some progress worth knowing about.

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:47.880
NTPC announced a $178 billion investment program running to 2037 aimed at increasing electricity generation.

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:52.780
The company is state controlled and supplies a large share of India's power.

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:59.540
Reuters reports the program will add both solar, wind and thermal capacity across multiple states.

00:06:59.540 --> 00:07:05.320
Officials present the plan as a response to projected demand growth over the next decade.

00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:11.460
The long-time frame means actual spending will depend on future budgets and technology choices.

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:18.960
One complication is balancing new coal capacity with renewable goals already set by the national government.

00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:27.900
Reuters coverage shows the investment is expected to create thousands of construction and operations jobs in regions where new plants are cited.

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:35.520
What happens next, job creation figures and capacity additions will be tracked against the national non-fossil target.

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:40.560
Now, to really understand this story, there's something most coverage leaves out.

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:47.260
Most coverage of charges against a messaging app founder treats the case as a simple law enforcement step.

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:53.320
Yet the underlying mechanism is a clash between national jurisdiction and global infrastructure.

00:07:54.320 --> 00:08:01.760
When a country issues an international wanted list for a founder whose company is incorporated elsewhere and whose servers are distributed,

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:08.320
it relies on extradition treaties and financial pressure points rather than direct control of the code.

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:13.340
The last several decades show that such actions rarely shut platforms down.

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:20.760
Instead, they prompt companies to adjust moderation policies or relocate staff while encryption remains intact.

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:28.180
Similar cases involving other encrypted services have led to negotiated settlements rather than outright bans.

00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:33.840
Knowing this, when reports emphasize either terrorism crack down or free speech attack,

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:41.420
readers can check whether the specific charge names particular messages or merely the general refusal to install backdoors.

00:08:41.420 --> 00:08:50.340
A concrete detail to verify next time is whether the charging document lists individual user accounts or simply the company's architecture.

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:57.540
Another useful step is to compare the exact wording of the indictment with statements issued by the company's legal team.

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:03.680
If you want to go deeper on today's lead, compare how AP news and Reuters are covering it.

00:09:04.180 --> 00:09:06.920
You will see two different frames on the same facts.

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:13.460
Tomorrow, watch for the US Federal Reserve's policy announcement and any immediate market reaction.

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:20.720
That's omnivue, as always, compare outlets, look for primary documents and separate what's known from what's assumed.

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:27.320
The world is complicated, but it's not beyond understanding and you just spent a few minutes understanding it better.

